  "doc": [
    "*Vespa crabro*, male, dorsal — the European hornet.",
    "",
    "Traced from figure 1 of Robert Morgan's plate 20 for Edward Saunders's *The",
    "Hymenoptera Aculeata of the British Islands* (1896);",
    "`references/vespa-crabro.jpg` is the sheet and the hornet is the large figure",
    "at the top. The plate's own caption names the sex, which is why this plate",
    "does: a male hornet has longer antennae and one more antennal segment than a",
    "worker, and both show from above.",
    "",
    "## The waist is the order",
    "",
    "`REQUIRED_PARTS.hymenoptera` does not list a part for it, and deliberately:",
    "the waist is the *gap* between the thorax and the abdomen rather than a thing",
    "to draw. What that means for an author is that the two parts either side of",
    "it have to be measured honestly — a thorax drawn a little long or an abdomen",
    "a little forward closes the gap, and a wasp without a waist is a fly.",
    "",
    "## Bands, and why they are markings rather than segments",
    "",
    "Both are drawn, and they are not the same thing. `abdomen-segment` is the",
    "join between one tergite and the next — an anatomical line that would be",
    "there on a plain brown wasp. The dark `marking` bands are the pattern, and on",
    "*crabro* they are notched: each runs across the tergite and then sends a",
    "point back down the midline. A key asks about the pattern; a reader counting",
    "segments needs the lines.",
    "",
    "## Wings",
    "",
    "Four, and all four declare `opacity: 'membrane'`. A hornet's wings are glass:",
    "the hindwing lies under the forewing and the legs pass under both, and",
    "painting them opaque would delete a leg apiece.",
    "",
    "## Plate space",
    "",
    "y = 0 at the top of the head, y = 1000 at the tip of the abdomen. The wings",
    "reach past x = 900 — nearly twice the length of the body either side — and",
    "the antennae above y = -190."
  ],
